# Supply Chain Provenance

Guarantees that every artifact produced by this pipeline is **traceable**, **signed**, and **reproducible** — shutting down the three most common modern supply chain attack vectors.

## How it works

Three controls run in sequence after all security scans pass:

| Step | Tool | What it proves |
|---|---|---|
| **SBOM Generation** | Syft | Every component in the artifact is listed and auditable |
| **Artifact Signing** | Cosign (keyless) | The artifact has not been tampered with since signing |
| **SLSA Provenance** | GitHub Attestations | The artifact was built by this exact workflow from this exact commit |

### Why this order matters

The supply chain provenance job runs **after** secrets-scan, gosec, and allowed-dependencies. You cannot sign a build that contains leaked secrets or unapproved packages — that would certify a compromised artifact.

## Attack vectors shut down

| Attack | How it's blocked |
|---|---|
| Dependency confusion | SBOM exposes any unexpected package. Allowed-dependencies enforcer blocks unapproved packages before signing. |
| Artifact tampering | Cosign signature is invalidated if a single byte changes post-signing. |
| Build pipeline compromise | SLSA provenance ties the artifact to the exact workflow SHA — a rogue build can't produce a valid attestation. |
| AI hallucinated packages | Packages AI tools invent won't be in `allowed-dependencies.yaml` and can't pass the SBOM gate. |
| Phantom package injection | Any package appearing in the SBOM but not the allowlist fails the pipeline before signing. |

## Signing: no key management

Cosign keyless signing uses the **GitHub Actions OIDC token** as the signing identity. There is no private key to:
- Rotate
- Store securely
- Accidentally leak

The signature is tied to: `https://github.com/<org>/<repo>/.github/workflows/<workflow>.yml@refs/heads/<branch>`

All signatures are recorded in the **Rekor transparency log** — a public, append-only, tamper-evident ledger.

## Verifying an artifact (anyone can do this)

```bash
# Verify SBOM signature
cosign verify-blob sbom-source.spdx.json \
  --signature sbom-source.spdx.json.sig \
  --certificate sbom-source.spdx.json.pem \
  --certificate-identity-regexp "https://github.com/Faslanrizni/.*" \
  --certificate-oidc-issuer "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"

# Verify SLSA provenance attestation
gh attestation verify sbom-source.spdx.json \
  --repo Faslanrizni/security-guardrails-faslan
```

## Policy

Governed by `policies/supply-chain.yaml`. Three settings:

```yaml
supply_chain:
  sbom: required             # fails build if SBOM not generated
  artifact_signing: required # fails build if Cosign signing fails
  provenance: required       # fails build if attestation not created
```

Change `required` → `optional` to warn without blocking.

## CI permissions required

The supply chain workflow needs elevated permissions not required by other guardrails:

```yaml
permissions:
  id-token: write      # Request OIDC token for keyless signing
  attestations: write  # Store provenance in GitHub Attestations API
  packages: write      # Push signed images to GHCR (if image signing used)
  contents: write      # Upload attestation artifacts
```

## Artifacts retained

All supply chain evidence is uploaded as `supply-chain-evidence` and retained for **90 days** (vs 30 days for other scans) to satisfy typical compliance audit windows.

Files included:
- `sbom-source.spdx.json` — SPDX Software Bill of Materials
- `sbom-source.cdx.json` — CycloneDX Software Bill of Materials  
- `sbom-source.spdx.json.sig` — Cosign signature
- `sbom-source.spdx.json.pem` — Cosign certificate (includes identity + OIDC issuer)
- `provenance-report.json` — Machine-readable control pass/fail summary

## SLSA Level

This implementation achieves **SLSA Level 2**:
- ✅ Version controlled source
- ✅ Hosted build platform (GitHub Actions)
- ✅ Signed provenance (GitHub Attestations)

SLSA Level 3 (hermetic, isolated build) would require additional hardening of the build environment itself.